Q: How can I create a struct in C which can hold any datatype inside it? A: <blockquote>
<p>How can I create a struct in C which can hold any datatype inside it?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>One could begin with:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>struct any {
  enum identifier;
  union {
    bool b;
    char c;
    int i;
    long long ll;
    float f;
    complex long double cld;
    void * vp;
    int (*fp)();
  } u;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Yet there is still <code>signed char</code>, <code>size_t</code>, and many other C types.</p>
<p>C is <em>type rich</em>.</p>
<p>With C23, we can have <code>_BitInt(1)</code>, <code>_BitInt(2)</code>, <code>_BitInt(3)</code> ... <code>_BitInt(BITINT_MAXWIDTH)</code> where <code>BITINT_MAXWIDTH</code> might be quite large like 65535.</p>
<p>So I think  <em>any datatype inside</em> is not a reasonable goal.</p>

