Q: Adding new Flag to urfave CLI Tool Breaks Existing Flag A: <p>The reason <code>excludeFlag</code> no longer works is because the <code>excludeFlag</code> was replaced with the &quot;file flag&quot; in the array of Flags.</p>
<p>You can fix it in 2 ways:</p>
<ol>
<li><p>Provide <code>fileFlag</code> as an option in the parent command so that <code>up</code> sub-command can inherit it. Refer to the snippet below.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Provide it in the array of <code>cli.Flag</code>:  <code>[]cli.Flag{fileFlag, excludeFlag}</code></p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>I have opted to set the <code>Destination</code> variable for the Flags so that the parser sets their value.</p>
<pre class="lang-golang prettyprint-override"><code>
func main() {
    var composeFile string
    var excludedServices []string

    excludeFlag := &amp;cli.StringSliceFlag{
        Name:    &quot;exclude&quot;,
        Aliases: []string{&quot;e&quot;},
        Usage:   &quot;services to exclude&quot;,
        Destination: &amp;excludedServices,
    }

    fileFlag := &amp;cli.StringFlag{
        Name:    &quot;file&quot;,
        Aliases: []string{&quot;f&quot;},
        Value: &quot;docker-compose.yaml&quot;, // Default file
        Usage:   &quot;--file &lt;target docker compose file&gt;&quot;,
        Destination: &amp;composeFile,
    }

    //...
    app := &amp;cli.Command{
        Name:  &quot;Dockexclude&quot;,
        //...
        Flags: []cli.Flag{
            fileFlag,
        },
        Commands: []*cli.Command{
            {
                Name:      &quot;up&quot;,
                Usage:     &quot;Start and create containers&quot;,
                UsageText: &quot;dockexclude up [--exclude &lt;service&gt;...],
                Flags:     []cli.Flag{excludeFlag},
                Action: func(ctx context.Context, cmd *cli.Command) error {
                    // here you can forward the composeFile variable to runDockerCompose
                    runDockerCompose(&quot;up&quot;, excludedServices)
                    return nil
                },
            },

//...
</code></pre>
